--- body ---
## Context

`AGENTS.md` section 3 lists the paths an unattended run may edit and says any
edit outside the set must open the PR as a draft and stop the merge. In one
raid (`/raid 255 256 278 279`) it fired four times:

| ticket | file | verdict |
|--------|------|---------|
| 0279 | `docs/erg-manual.md` | **wrong** — the file is generated, and `tests/test_docs.sh` *fails CI* if it is stale. No ticket touching help text could satisfy both rules. |
| 0279 | `src/go/assets/integration.md` | **right** — hand-written prose shipped to adopters. Author read the diff and approved. |
| 0255 | `CHANGELOG.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md` | **wrong** — repo-internal docs the project's own conventions require updating for exactly this class of change. `README.md`, the same kind of file, is already on the list. |
| 0255 | `tickets/erg-github` | **wrong** — the list has `tickets/*.erg`, which does not match an extensionless helper. Every ticket touching the forge layer hits this. |

Three of the four were false positives, and each arose the same way: the
project's own conventions require an edit the list forbids.

## Why removal rather than a wider list

**Nothing enforces it.** `CI.yml` runs `make test` and `make validate`; no job
reads the allowlist. It is unexecuted prose, so its only effect is on an agent
that reads and obeys it — which means it slows down the careful and does
nothing about the careless.

**Its remedy is already the default.** Section 3's answer to an
out-of-allowlist edit is "a human reads the diff before merging". That is what
a pull request is. The gate adds a second, weaker copy of review, gated on a
path list that nobody keeps current.

**Growing the list does not converge.** 0284 added `docs/erg-manual.md` and the
gate fired twice more the same day on different files. The membership was never
the problem: any list that must anticipate every file a convention-following
change touches will keep being wrong, and each miss costs an author decision.

**The one true positive needed no list.** `integration.md` is shipped prose,
and it got a human ruling — through the PR, which is where that judgement
belongs. Nothing about that outcome required a path allowlist to exist.

`rules/workflow.md` states the general form: in a tooling repo, when a guard
misfires, prefer deleting the guard and its tests over growing them.

## Actions

1. Delete `AGENTS.md` section 3 and renumber the sections that follow (4-7
   become 3-6).
2. Remove the `diff remains inside allowlist` bullet from the merge gate.
3. Remove `CONTRIBUTING.md`'s "Keep the diff inside the change-scope allowlist"
   line.
4. Leave the safety gate (section 2, hard no-go for unattended work: secrets,
   infra, permissions) untouched. That gate names *kinds of action* that are
   genuinely unsafe unattended, not a path list, and it has not misfired.

## Test

No test to add: there was never a test. That is part of the finding — the gate
was prose, so its removal cannot break a check, and its presence never
guaranteed one. `make check` must still pass, which verifies only that nothing
else referenced it.

## Verification

- [x] `grep -rn "allowlist\|change-scope"` finds no live reference outside
      historical ticket bodies
- [x] Section numbering is contiguous after the removal
- [x] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- The safety gate stays. Removing a path list is not removing the rule against
  unattended secret handling, infra changes, or permission-model edits.
- PR review remains mandatory. This ticket removes a redundant second gate, not
  the first one.

## Exit criteria

- The change-scope allowlist no longer exists in `AGENTS.md` or `CONTRIBUTING.md`
- No section references it

## Related

- 0284 — added `docs/erg-manual.md` to the list. It fixed a real
  unsatisfiable-rule collision, but treated the instance; this ticket treats
  the class. 0284's reasoning about generated-and-drift-tested files is
  superseded, not contradicted.
